  "notes": "TRANSITIVITY:\nTransitive ({他動詞|たどうし}). The intransitive pair is {替|か}わる.\n\nASPECT (ている):\n{替|か}えている indicates an ongoing action of replacing, or that a replacement has been made. The focus is typically on the result.\n\nWRITING:\nJapanese has several かえる verbs with different kanji, each with a distinct nuance:\n- {替|か}える: to replace one thing with another of the same kind (e.g., changing clothes, swapping a battery)\n- {変|か}える: to change the nature or state of something (e.g., changing a plan, altering a schedule)\n- {代|か}える: to substitute one person or thing for another (e.g., a substitute teacher)\n- {換|か}える: to exchange or convert (e.g., exchanging currency, converting units)\nIn practice, these distinctions are often blurred, and hiragana かえる is frequently used.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {服|ふく}を{替|か}える: to change clothes\n- {電池|でんち}を{替|か}える: to replace batteries\n- {水|みず}を{替|か}える: to change the water\n- {気分|きぶん}を{替|か}える: to change one's mood",
